what's up everybody welcome back to my  channel Richard on data this is my data  science statistics tutorial series where  I give you all the applied statistics  knowledge that you need to conquer the  data science world so in my last video I  talked about measures of center the mean  the median and the mode these are all  ways we can describe the central  tendency of a variable however in real  life it's often not enough to do that  you need to describe the spread of a  variable  maybe the uncertainty of some kind of  estimate and generally just describing  and reducing uncertainty and spread that  is the foundation for an awful lot of  applied statistics make sure you watch  this video to the end because I'm gonna  go over five different metrics  that's range interquartile range mean  absolute deviation standard deviation  and variance I'm gonna talk about how  you calculate these things how they  generally manifest and show up in  practice and then some of the awesome  intuition and properties behind standard  deviation and variance before I do that  though smash the like button hit the  subscribe button and then hit the  notification bell so YouTube notifies  you whenever I upload a new video all  right so let's start with a small amount  of data now let's just pretend these are  temperatures in the month of March in  Detroit we're gonna do that because I  like weather and because the weather  here gets really really weird sometimes  you're gonna see the data down below on  the screen and yes I exaggerated these a  little bit but only barely let's start  with a few summary metrics here so the  mean is 43.8 going down the list a  little bit you've got a minimum of 15  you've got a 25th percentile or 25th  quantile also known as the first  quartile that's 30 2.25 the median is 43  the 75th percentile is 55 point two five  and the max is seventy one right away  all those numbers except for the mean  that's just always a nice to have  or a metric those other numbers together  comprise your five number summary and  you can tell from looking at these right  away you already have a pretty  reasonable feel for the distribution of  this data and the general spread and  shape of it but then if you take your  minimum that's 15 and your maximum 71  then you take the difference between  them you get 56 and that's your range  now this number is not incredibly useful  in practice because if you think about a  real-world variable in a real world data  set you're gonna have a lot of outliers  on both ends so practically if you're  just taking the difference between the  maximum and the minimum  you're just taking the difference  between whatever the most extreme  outliers you have on either side are so  it's a nice to know thing it's probably  not something you're gonna end up using  and practice a whole lot though but  let's go back to the twenty-fifth  percentile and the 75th percentile  though so remember those are thirty two  point two five and fifty five point two  five and the difference between them is  twenty-three now that difference between  q1 and q3 that is the interquartile  range now this is a bit better of a  descriptor of the variability of your  data than the ranges because already you  have some kind of intuition and feeling  for what the middle 50% of your data  behave like it also has a nice practical  property because if you take the formula  twenty-fifth percentile minus 1.5 times  IQR that gives you the lower end cutoff  for outliers in the data set or at least  it's one way that outliers can be  defined similarly if you take the 75th  percentile or q3 plus 1.5 times  interquartile range that gives you the  threshold for outliers on the high side  so just another nice property and it's a  nice way in which interquartile range  can actually get used and practice and  be meaningful next I'm going to tell you  about a metric you've probably never  even heard of before and that's called  the mean absolute deviation and it is  calculated pretty much exactly like how  its  so you're gonna take the deviation or  the difference between every single data  point and the mean so if you take the  low end point here 15 the mean is forty  three point eight the deviation or  difference between those two  it's just twenty eight point eight the  direction doesn't matter because we're  just taking the absolute value here  you're gonna take every single one of  those deviations or differences and take  the average of all those that is called  the main absolute deviation and it's  fourteen point one in this particular  set of data more on this in a little bit  next one is the one everybody's heard of  before at least and that's the standard  deviation now this one is actually very  similar to the main absolute deviation  but it's the one everybody uses in  practice and this one is interpreted  basically as approximately the average  distance that an observation is from the  mean and the way this is calculated is  somewhat similar but different how it's  actually calculated is you take every  distance an observation is from the main  you square it then you add all those  squared deviations up you have a sum of  squares you take the square root of that  and then divide by n minus one now on a  technical note there's actually a  difference between what's called the  population standard deviation and the  sample standard deviation in the  population version we divide by n in the  denominator in the sample version we  divide by n minus one in the denominator  there's not really a practical  difference between these two very often  the difference is for mathematical  reasons but usually we'll use the N  minus 1 or the sample version because we  don't have a full populations worth of  data we have just a samples worth of  data now there's actually a real  practical benefit to us using the  standard deviation instead of the main  absolute deviation which we almost  always do in practice and that's because  with the standard deviation we're  squaring a whole bunch of things and we  add them up we create a sum of squares  eventually we take a square root but  that process of squaring things by  definition when you square things you  are making things bigger you're creating  more deviation you're creating more  distance the process of doing that  actually penalizes outliers more and if  you have a lot of outliers you end up  seeing a much bigger gap between the  standard deviation and what the main  absolute deviation would be so if you  take our example from before the mean  absolute deviation was 14 point 1 but  the standard deviation is seventeen  point three so just keep this in mind  the mean absolute deviation is a little  bit sensitive to outliers the standard  deviation significantly more so and a  lot of practical applied statistics and  data science work it can really Center  on reducing variability reducing  variance reducing the standard deviation  what have you so I hope this really goes  to illustrate the impact that outliers  can have and sometimes when that's your  goal of reducing uncertainty or reducing  variation outliers are the very first  thing that you want to think about last  but not least we have variance so this  actually gets misused in people's  language a lot variance is actually a  very specific statistical term sometimes  it can be used interchangeably by people  with variation or variability which are  more of these general terms which  describe the spread or uncertainty about  your data no don't use those  interchangeably with variance because  variance is a very specific term  variance does not have a super intuitive  definition it's approximately the  average squared distance that an  observation is away from the mean it's  calculated by very similarly to what we  did with the standard deviation we take  every observation subtract it from the  mean square that and add all those  squared differences up then divide by n  minus 1 the only difference is in the  numerator we're not taking a square root  anymore they're often going to see  instead of the standard deviation used  alongside the mean to describe a  statistical distribution now this is  just because it has really awesome  mathematical properties it's also known  as the second moment of a distribution  and the same property as before with the  standard deviation applies with the  sample variance versus the population  variance so in practice of all the  metrics I've just described  you're going to see the standard  deviation manifest a lot more than any  of the others most of the time and just  really notice from that one number the  standard deviation look how much more  information than just the mean alone you  have about that one variable again  you're getting all this more information  about what the data look like from only  one number standard deviation and  variance form the mathematical backbone  of statistics because almost all of  statistics really does come down to  quantifying how uncertain or how  unlikely some particular event is  statistics is the science for that and  these are really just the building  blocks for that science I really can't  stress enough so much of practical  applied data science comes down to  reducing or eliminating uncertainty so I  really hope this provided some intuition  behind that and just help to provide  some helpful food for thought so thanks  for watching this video until next time  Richard on data
 

